[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged by subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject))
and marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Its own cause table already disagrees: one of its two causes
is marked "not this ticket — no ticket of its own", and a footnote holds reports that are not a month/day
swap at all. Read by what broke, #4471 holds three problems. Each one now has its own ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
Fixing the parser closes only the first row. The other two need their own fixes.
</Callout>

## The split

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} tickets={{ parser: `${repo}/1`, hint: `${repo}/2`, shift: `${repo}/3` }} />

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads dates month first; every tenant on file writes the day first.
  "7 April" comes out as "4 July". This is the bug #4471 describes.
- **`hint`**: `booking-v3` prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People at day-first tenants type
  what the hint says and the form reads it day first. Same symptom, different code: the parser fix
  does not touch it.
- **`shift`**: the booking lands one day off, or on a different day for people in different time zones.
  The month never moves. The desk already read these as one-day moves (`desk-notes.json`), but the
  table filed them under `parser` by tenant. This looks like a time-zone conversion bug.

## Outcome

Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October:

- **`parser`**: fixed, `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`. [#1](https://github.com/Brafit2001/support-kb-4471/issues/1) closed as completed.
- **`hint`**: won't fix, the `booking-v3` form is retired. [#2](https://github.com/Brafit2001/support-kb-4471/issues/2) closed as not planned.
- **`shift`**: the desk files these reports under `parser` by tenant. [#3](https://github.com/Brafit2001/support-kb-4471/issues/3)
  closed as a duplicate of #1. If one-day moves keep coming in after the parser fix, reopen it.

## How the reports were placed

- **Count each report once.** The ticket export comes in overlapping pages, so some reports appear twice.
  Every count here is by unique report id.
- **Place by tenant, not by intake tag.** The intake tag is guessed from the reporter's browser. The cause
  depends on the tenant's locale and booking form (`causes.ts`, `locales.ts`).
- **Read before merging.** A matching subject says two people chose the same words, not that they hit the
  same bug. Reports with the same subject sit in all three rows.

## What #4471 shows today

For comparison, the table on the incident page, built from the same data:

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## Lessons

- A count is only as good as the rule that merged it. Merge by cause, not by subject.
- When a table says "no ticket of its own", open one.

